<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! Mixed-model swarm ~ many lineages, one hand per lane

The house works across several model lineages at once. Each lineage holds the seat it suits, every seat keeps the same lane law, and one hand per lane lands the work. A second lineage earns its place by seeing what the first one misses, never by adding a writer.

The crew lines that bind a running lane stand at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/controller-handoff`>> #/the-lane-law; a spirit's mask, lifecycle and handback form stand at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/noosphere-boot`>> #/worker-swarm.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-seats`>>
!! The seats ~ which lineage holds what

|! Seat |! Lineage |! Holds |! Never |
| ''Operator'' | the human | rulings, credentials, the operator-only configs (`~/.codex/config.toml`, `~/.claude/settings.json`, `~/.copilot/*`, gh auth), WSL size, every founding and custody act on a TTY, the `--force` on a lane worktree that refuses a plain remove | answers a question canon, a measure or the stories settle |
| ''Controller'' | Claude Opus | the session's seat: lane briefs, slot fill, the one watcher per seat, a read of every chunk's diff before it lands, the batch land, the road commit behind it, canon | solos a lane; lands a chunk it has not read |
| ''Lane lead'' (Episkopos, a Wright) | Claude, named per lane (#/the-tiers) | the lane's briefs, the authoring of every red and every judgment slice, every commit in its own worktree on its lane branch, the build before each seat, the batch battery, one push per feature, the short hand-back | pushes the span or `main`; arms a watcher; launches a seat on a stale dist |
| ''Terra'' (builder) | Codex `gpt-6.1-terra`, medium effort | a mechanical edit over an exact file list and an exact change; typecheck; single-file unit runs that open no socket | commits, builds, opens a socket, writes outside its seat directory and the worktree |
| ''Luna'' (reader, sweeper) | Codex `gpt-6-luna`, high effort, read-only | the cold read of each batch before its push, the aftermath sweep | builds, commits, reads a diff it wrote |
| ''Sol'' (deep reader) | Codex `gpt-6.1-sol`, high effort, read-only | the read of every batch head, every caps, custody, crypto, gate or Keyhive slice, a named deep question | builds by default, commits, launches as `gpt-6-sol` |
| ''Copilot seat'' | Copilot `gpt-5.3-codex`, medium effort | the batch Aftermath read while the legacy allowance lasts | builds, commits, reads a diff it wrote, runs `--model fusion` |
| ''Fresh reader'' | a lineage that built nothing in the piece, read-only | an adversarial read of a whole lane before its train, findings ranked with `file:line` | sees the builder's conclusions first |
| ''Research pair'' | two seats woken apart | the code read and the field read of one question, each claim marked MEASURED or LEAD, then traded | shares a conclusion with its twin before the trade |

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-shapes`>>
!! The shapes ~ how a crew stands

* ''The work swarm.'' A lane runs one lead with the smallest crew that finishes the slice: Terra on file-listed edits, Luna and Sol reading, the Copilot seat reading the batch's Aftermath. Terra, Luna, Sol and `gpt-5.3-codex` share the OpenAI lineage, so their cross-read counts once; the lead reads every build as the second lineage, and the controller reads every chunk. A seat never reads a diff it wrote.
* ''The build pair.'' The building seats share ONE worktree the lead provisions, with ONE writer at a time, so their work composes as it lands. A seat that writes in parallel works in a worktree of its own, and the lead merges from it.
* ''A short lineage keeps its seat.'' When the operator names a lineage short, its seats pass to another lineage's models of the same capability, and the lens law still sorts who reads whom. Where Codex stands absent, a lane runs an Opus lead who authors the code, with a Sonnet helper taking listed edits and a Sonnet or Opus spirit reading and sweeping.
* ''Crew law.'' Every lane keeps these:
** A controller ruling on behavior carries a measure (a grep, one test it ran) or arrives marked LEAN; the lane verifies before building and pushes back with evidence.
** The lead authors every red and every judgment slice; Terra receives an exact file list and an exact change. After one thin pass on judgment work, the lead weighs a Sol seat before a second Terra brief.
** The red lands as its own commit before its fix, so a reader sees the red's sha fail on its parent; the commit message quotes the failure, and a control ships beside it. A red written after its fix names that and carries a proof run against the parent. Phase seats wrap this order: reds seat, the lead commits and proves the red, fix seat, the lead commits.
** A seat whose run fails, gets cancelled or misses its red lands nothing; the lead discards its partial diff by name.
** After a reader's findings the builder revises ONCE; a second round of the same findings goes to the lead, then the controller.
** A reader's findings go straight to the builder's lead; the controller reads the copy and rules only on forks (design, scope, law), settled by <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/working-procedures`>> #/forks.
** One writer per worktree; no claims ledger.
** The controller arms ONE watcher per seat (`until [ -f <seat>/final.md ]`, in the harness's background mode); a lead hands back once its seat launches and the controller's watcher wakes it. Leads and seats arm none. The lead keeps `resume.md` current at each commit and before each launch, and the controller sweeps a landed lane's watchers by pid.
** A lead hands back ONCE, short: one `<<~ status "…">>` line, the head (full sha), the CI and held-out readings on it, the story and the walk that proved it, the rulings taken from canon each with its source, and the true operator asks each with a lean. A failed or discarded seat gets a leg line (directory, model, effort, mode, attempts, outcome). Spend numbers come from the controller's `spend.py`, never a seat's word.
** Spend reads from what each tool writes: Claude subagent transcripts, Codex rollouts, the Copilot run log's `Requests N Premium` line. A Copilot log with no such line reads UNMETERED, never zero; the Codex pool holds a weekly floor read from the newest rollout (20% for a build or research seat, 0% for a read seat, the operator letting readers spend the pool to its end for now). `spend.py` serves the controller alone and binds no lane.
* ''Observe pair.'' Before a phase designs anything, one seat cold-reads the as-built with `file:line`, and a second, woken apart and handed the framing alone, reads the field. Their handbacks collide in orient.
* ''The Chao of a review'' (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/mu/chao`>>). Two reads over one question wear the Chao's two faces, and the review resolves to three:
** ''Hodge'' carries Yang and Ha, the Aneristic face: what holds its shape. It reads what the work states as standing (each claim, name and contract) against the source, and finds the line that does not hold.
** ''Podge'' carries Yin and Ka, the Eristic face: what moves. It reads what the work does under change (the race, the other caller, the epoch that rolls, the case left out, the control that never bites).
** ''Spin'' carries Ba. Each face reads alone first; then the pair trades: each takes the other's findings, confirms or refutes each against the source, and reads the other's ground for what its own face missed. Orthogonal findings both stand; tensioned ones get measured, never voted; a finding one face alone raised stays a lead.
* ''Overwatch.'' The controller reads every crew's handback and every chunk's diff before it lands, and names each finding it would block on. Work that moves a fact between processes or threads (a writer and its readers, a composer and its islands, a verb and its route) takes the controller's own read of the crossing, since a module's own reads catch its contract and miss its crossings.
* ''The fresh read and the train.'' A lane's handback takes a fresh reader's whole-lane read before the batch lands; a HOLD sends the findings back as the next launch, up to five launches, with the uncertainties talked out between them. The controller then lands ready lanes in batches (#/the-lanes).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-seat-law`>>
!! The seat law ~ the brief section every Codex seat carries

`tools/launch-codex.mjs` preflights each Codex seat and appends the bullets below to its brief, once, reading them from this section. It refuses a model missing from Codex's models cache, `gpt-6-sol`, a pool at or under its weekly floor (20% for a build or research seat, 0% for a read seat, the operator letting readers spend the pool to its end for now), a pool line unreadable twice (never read as zero), a seat directory outside `~/.lares-scratch/<lane>/`, a build seat on the main checkout, and a build seat over a stale dist. It logs each attempt.

* A lane launches no Sol seat; the controller's one Sol read per train runs at the read floor of 0%.
* Stay inside the wall. Approval never escalates; a build seat has no network, loopback included; only a research seat takes `--search`. A step that needs more than the wall stops and names itself in `out.md`; the wall holds by refusal.
* Never commit and never run a git write. The lead commits each red, then each fix.
* Never build (`pnpm build`), never run vitest browser mode, never stand a vessel, socket or daemon. Typecheck (`tsc -p … --noEmit`) and run single test files that open no socket; mark any test that needs a socket "lead runs".
* A "stale build" from the dist guard stops the run; name it, and the lead builds.
* Write `out.md` and every note in the seat directory alone. `-o` names `final.md`; stdin closes (`< /dev/null`); `TMPDIR` and `TMPPREFIX` sit inside the seat directory.
* Never run the battery; the lead holds its lock. Never read a credential file.
* Launch without `--ephemeral`: each seat keeps its rollout, so the spend tally counts it.
* The lead runs `pnpm build` in its worktree after every span merge and before each build seat; a verdict read off a stale dist counts as unread.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-feature-unit`>>
!! The feature unit ~ a PR lands one whole capability

# ''A PR lands one capability'': a story a person carries out end to end, on every vessel class it names, its security fence complete. Slices live inside the PR as red and fix pairs, each read by the lead and the batch's Luna read; a slice never opens a PR alone.
# ''Three units ship alone, fast'': a cure for a span fault that blocks other lanes; a porch hygiene bundle; a ground change later features build on, whole at its own layer with no stub and no deferred caller.
# ''A hole in the capability stays in the PR.'' A gap that makes the story fail or leaves its fence open gets cured before the land. Only a finding that widens scope past the story goes to the next feature's brief. The Aftermath read, and for a security-class feature the Sol read, land before the push.
# ''Split by capability, never by layer and never by the hole the next slice closes.'' A feature that outruns what one lead carries across two working sessions, or one writer per worktree, splits into two capabilities, each a whole story.
# ''One push per green fast tier, plus cure pushes.'' The lead pushes the lane branch once its fast tier reads green and its reds stand proven (#/the-lanes); it pushes again only to cure a red. It merges the span once, right before that push, where a conflict or a non-land span commit asks.
# ''A span fault stops the queue once.'' A red the span carries goes at once to ONE cure lane. Queued lanes push, rerun and re-merge nothing until the cure lands; then each merges once and pushes once. A failed job reruns ONCE as a flake candidate; a second red on the same test names a fault.
# ''A feature waits on its dependency unbuilt, or stacks.'' A feature that needs another lane's land starts after that land, or stacks on that lane's branch and lands in the same batch. It never builds ahead and rests while the span moves.
# ''Done means the story walks.'' The hand-back names the story and the one walk that proves it: the one e2e file, or a live stand where a vessel class carries it. A capability no walk proves stands "specified", never built (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/working-procedures`>> #/wiring-an-unreached-module).
# ''The record rides with the capability'': each feature PR hands the controller its changelog bullet and road-row text; the lane edits neither file.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-tiers`>>
!! The tiers ~ each seat draws the tier that serves it

* ''Controller'': Opus 5.5, high effort, refreshing its context at each batch boundary. Fable takes a named ruling Opus at max visibly missed.
* ''Lane lead'': Sonnet 5.5 with the one-hour subagent cache by default; Opus 5.5 for a seam, custody or security lane, the reason written in the brief. A lead retires at its PR's land and a fresh seat resumes from a cold brief.
* ''Fable as a burst'': a Fable lead takes a named integration-judgment bottleneck when all four hold: the lead authors the code; one lane-day at most; no wait past an hour (on a longer wait it writes a brief, hands back and retires); the weekly Fable pool holds room. Fable never stands as a lane's standing lead.
* ''Builder'': Terra 6.1 medium on file-listed edits alone. A `gpt-6.1-sol` build seat answers a named ask. No Opus or Fable builder seat.
* ''Reader'': Luna high reads every batch. Sol 6.1 high reads once per TRAIN, over the merged head before the batch lands, covering every lane in it; a lane launches no Sol seat, a security-class slice included. A security-class slice takes the controller's own diff read and a Copilot cold read. A fresh Claude reader serves where the read needs house canon; Fable cold-reads only a spec with open forks that an expensive builder will inherit.
* ''Researcher'': Fable for parallel web collides, Opus for repo-grounded research, Sonnet for audits and sweeps, Codex Sol with `--search` for one question.
* ''General-purpose subagents'' run Sonnet unless the roster names Opus or Fable.

The measures behind each placement stand at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/model-tiers-2026`>>.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-battery`>>
!! The battery ~ the local run and the verdict

* ''A lane's CI makes its verdict; held-out runs per train.'' `.github/workflows/held-out.yml` fires on the span, `main`, the weekly clock and by hand, never on a lane push; a push that changes only meme, docs or wiki paths (`tools/ci-ignored-paths.json`, read by both workflows' `paths-ignore` and by the train's road guard) raises no run and cancels none, and the guard carries such a road commit over its nearest green ancestor; a lane reads GREEN on its CI alone, its held-out reading prints but never gates (`held-out: not run (per train)` where no run stands), and the vigil on the span reads both after each land. The lane's local battery reads what it touched, in this order: `pnpm build`; each red's proof against its parent; `node tools/evergreen-boundary.mjs`; `bash tools/witness-all.sh --static` (or `--fast`, the FAST tier: `--static` minus `contract-witness`, `custody-census-witness` and `law-mutants-witness`, which the long tier and the train run; the docker witnesses, `household-https` and `household-resolver`, leave the lane battery; CI and the train run the whole roster);  `tools/typecheck-witness.sh`; the touched packages' related tests (vitest `--changed` or `related`, never the whole suite) under a scratch `HOME` and a scratch `LAR_ROOT` with `LAR_BAGS` unset; `node --test` over the touched tools; ONE e2e file that exercises the touched surface, named in the hand-back.
* ''Two tiers.'' The FAST tier runs on the box with no lock: `pnpm build`, each red's proof against its parent, `tools/typecheck-witness.sh`, `node tools/evergreen-boundary.mjs`, `bash tools/witness-all.sh --fast` and each touched package's related tests (operator ruling, 2026-11-01: //"Approve the fast-tier lever"//), with no e2e. The LONG tier runs in the cloud on the lane's push and again on the train: every whole package suite, every e2e set, the slow witnesses, the docker witnesses and held-out.
* ''CI carries the rest'': the whole e2e sets and every other suite, and reads the witness fleet again as the verdict.
* ''One heavy battery at a time on the box.'' The fast tier's unit suites and static checks run without `tools/battery.sh`; the lock holds for the train and any heavy local run. The lead runs a heavy battery through `tools/battery.sh`, whose lock reads `LARES_BATTERY_LOCK`, else the passwd home's `.lares-scratch/.battery.lock` (never `$HOME`, since a lead runs under a scratch `HOME`); it refuses (exit 64) where the lock cannot open, never runs unlocked, and passes the command's exit code through.
* ''A red that clears with no edit names shared state'' (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/working-procedures`>> #/red-first).
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-lens-law`>>

!! The lens law ~ why several eyes outweigh one

# ''Correlated eyes count once.'' Two seats of one lineage share their blind spots; a finding both report still reads as one lead. A second lineage reads with a different prior, so its agreement carries weight and its dissent marks where to look.
# ''One eye alone yields a lead, never ground.'' Ground needs a second eye through another lens, or a measurement.
# ''The reader built nothing.'' A reviewer that built the piece reads its own intent into the diff.
# ''Framing without conclusion.'' A second researcher gets the question and the framing, never the first one's answer.
# ''A clean read clears nothing.'' A reader's finding earns a look, and its silence lands nothing. The lead re-derives any list a seat proposes to destroy, and a second eye reads the script.
# ''Measure over agree.'' Where eyes disagree, a probe, a red test or a repeated run settles it; a vote never does.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-copilot-seat`>>

!! The Copilot seat ~ Copilot's models in the swarm

GitHub Copilot reaches models the Codex budget never spends. Nothing the house gates on needs it to pass.

!!! Its uses
|! Use |! Model |! When |
| ''the batch's Aftermath reader'' | `gpt-5.3-codex`, medium effort | while the legacy allowance lasts; a fresh same-family Claude reader serves after |
| a deeper single read | a higher-multiplier model | where the seats split on a load-bearing finding, the operator hearing the reason first |
| ''Copilot code review'' of a pull request | GitHub's PR reviewer | only when the operator asks for one by name |
A premium request counts per prompt, never per tool call.

!!! Its fences
# ''One launcher'', `tools/launch-copilot.mjs read|build`, its walls pinned by `tools/launch-copilot.test.mjs`. Both modes run with no URL, no built-in MCP server (the GitHub server reaches the network and writes), no system temp directory, no prompt and no auto-update, stdin closed and `COPILOT_ALLOW_ALL` dropped; a seat whose working directory sits in the system temp directory refuses, since the temp wall blinds it there; `--model fusion` refuses. The seat's handback, log and share land in its seat directory.
# ''Read-only when it reads'' (`read`): every write, commit and push tool denied; git and file reads alone.
# ''Walled when it builds'' (`build`, where a build must go to Copilot): file writes in the lane's build worktree alone, `npx vitest` and `npx tsc` there; every git write, push, network fetch, `rm`, `mv`, `npm` and `pnpm` denied; it refuses the main checkout, a directory that holds no worktree, and a missing or in-checkout `LAR_ROOT`, refuses `--add-dir`, and drops any `LAR_BAGS`. A launch never widens these walls (no `shell(bash)`, no `shell(env)`, never `--allow-all-*` or `--yolo`).
# ''No laundering.'' A step this session's safety check refused never travels to Copilot, since routing a refused step through a second permission system counts as a retry.
# ''Spend reads from the run log's `Requests N Premium` line''; the entitlement cache reads last-known.
# ''Overage off.'' The allowance empties and stops; it resets on the first of each month and carries nothing over.
# ''No cloud agent.'' A cloud agent opens branches and pull requests under its own hand: outward-facing, and a second writer.
# ''Its config carries the house'': `~/.copilot/copilot-instructions.md` loads the boot seed, and `~/.copilot/hooks/lares.json` stands.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-lanes`>>
!! The lanes ~ a sub-branch, a pull request, a batch

* ''A lane opens its own sub-branch'' of the work branch, `feature/green-jello-dinosaur-5--<lane>`, cut from the span's head as it reads green. The lead commits there, in its own provisioned worktree, after `pnpm build`, with `git commit -F <msg> --only -- <paths>`; a subject reads `lar:///ha.ka.ba/<pkg-or-bag>/<area>/<w1.w2.w3> — summary`; a `Co-Authored-By` names each lineage that wrote lines, then the session line.
* ''A lane pushes its own branch'' once its fast tier reads green and its reds stand proven (`git push origin HEAD:refs/heads/<branch>`, never `-u`), then builds the next slice while CI runs; a red CI result becomes the next slice's first fix, and a lane never pushes the span or `main`. One push per green fast tier: a burst of small pushes cancels its predecessors' runs.
* ''One early pusher at a time.'' A full lane push raises about 46 jobs against an account that runs about 20 at once, so at most one lane pushes early beside the span; a second lane holds its push until the first lane's CI reads or 15 minutes pass, and the controller grants the slot in order. The land waits on green CI.
* ''A cloud bisect'' chases a single e2e failure with `node tools/e2e-shards.mjs push <lane>` from the lane's worktree (about 5 runners), never a local loop or a full push.
* ''A lane hands back on green'': its battery (#/the-battery), CI reads green on its head (held-out rides the train), its pull request opens into the work branch, and its fresh read precedes the land. A Copilot PR review runs only on the operator's ask.
* ''A lane worktree'' counts toward five at most (`node tools/provision-worktree.mjs <dir>` builds it whole in one run: submodules against the main checkout's objects, the frozen install, the grammar wasm, the TW5 vendor blob, every package build and the stamp); the train holds the sixth. Worktrees and scratch roots live under `~/.lares-scratch/<lane>/`, never `/tmp`; a provision reads `df` first and stops under 60G free on C:. A lane may hold a second worktree, each with one writer, when two features on its path build in parallel. Free slots fill from the road's order without asking.
* ''One tool lands a batch'': `tools/land-train.mjs` carries the train, the chained land, the direct land of a lane that already holds the span head, the disk floor (`LARES_DISK_MIN_C_GB`), the worktree cap, the span-push gate and the cancel of the landed lanes' runs; `--plan` prints the commits it would write and pushes nothing. A verdict reads three states: green, red, and UNREADABLE (GitHub unreachable or a 5xx), which waits, exits 10 and never reads as red or green; `tools/ci-verdict.mjs --wait` carries that wait, and its `job-minutes` line gives the CI currency below.
* ''The controller lands in batches'' (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/controller-handoff`>> #/state). A batch forms once two or more features read green; a lone green feature drains as a batch of one when no other lane has a feature nearing green, and a span-fault cure or shared ground other lanes build on lands at once. One train reads every green lane merged over the span as it stands: the shared packages' suites, `tools/witness-all.sh`, evergreen and the tallies (a suite log with no "Test Files" line reads red; a dirty tree reads red); each lane's CI carried the e2e. The land writes one commit per lane, chained, each with the train's merge tree, so the span's last tree equals the train's tree. The span pushes only while no run flies on it, and the land cancels the CI runs its lane-branch pushes start. A lane on an older base lands when the span moved only by lands since (subjects carrying ` — merge PR #N`).
* ''Road commits'' (road rows, o-takiage moves, changelog bullets, `#/state`) ride behind a batch, never between queued lands.
* ''After each land'' the build cache caps at 15GB (`docker builder prune -f --max-used-space 15GB`); no image or system prune without the operator's word.
* ''Job-minutes make the CI currency'': every CI change reports total job-minutes beside the critical path, and the fewest, fullest cells win while the critical path stays at or under the slowest e2e shard.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-otakiage`>>

!! The o-takiage ~ every lane closes by returning what it superseded (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/otakiage`>>)

A lane closes with an o-takiage pass before its pull request opens, and the controller closes each settle with one over everything it landed. The rite returns rather than discards: git keeps the ashes, and the tree reads as if the new shape had always stood.

# ''Burn only what a later carrier or module plainly superseded'': an old message kind, a dead dial, a scaffold test, a stale comment. Unwired work no landed lane superseded stays; doubt goes on an ask list.
# ''Move, then link.'' Where a new carrier gathers knowledge scattered across others, the scattered lines move into it with their attribution, each old site keeps one line that links the new home, and nothing states one law twice.
# ''Canon follows the code in the same burst.'' A census over every carrier names each burned symbol, file, flag and variable; each live line re-trues to what stands, citing file and symbol, never a line number.
# ''Testimony stays.'' A testimony carrier, a dated scout and a dated crossing row keep their words; the house re-trues their frame, never their body.
# ''A witness that catches burned names burns itself'' once it reads green over the tree.
# ''Roadmaps move what stands finished'' onto their own o-takiage shelf, verbatim, and keep only what stays owed on the road.
# ''Owed items travel first.'' A road row that names nothing still owed moves to `#/o-takiage`; an item owed for another row moves to that row before the move.
# ''Scratch returns too.'' A lane worktree leaves after a census proves every file it holds landed, by a plain `git worktree remove`; one that refuses plain removal waits for the operator's hand. The land script force-removes the train worktree it made, once it has proven the final tree equals the pushed span tree.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-fences`>>

!! The fences ~ every lineage keeps them

* ''One writer per lane.'' Seats of any lineage write only inside their own worktree; the lead alone commits, from its own tree.
* ''A refused command stops that step'' for every lineage alike: never retried, reshaped, split, moved to another tool, or handed to another seat (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/controller-handoff`>> #/the-lane-law).
* ''Every brief carries the git lines'': no `git stash` in any form, no `--no-verify`, no `.git/config`, no git write from a seat; `git config core.bare` reads `false` before and after.
* ''A build reads its own tree'': a provisioned checkout whose links resolve inside it.
* ''A process stops by its own pid'' or through the harness's task stop, never by a command-line pattern that matches the shell running it. Watchers too: a watcher reads a file by its modification time, never `pgrep -f`.
* ''The disk carries; the seat re-reads.'' A spirit runs outside its overseer's budget, so an outage (a rate limit, a crash, a compaction) stops the watcher and never the work: the spirit finishes its brief and its handback lands on disk unread, its build dirty in the worktree. The lead's resume sheet (named in the crew law) names the spirit's directory and `out.md`, so a re-awakened seat reads what the spirit left and never mistakes it for stray work.
* ''A bisect reads the head it names.'' `node tools/e2e-shards.mjs push <lane>` serves a bisect and pushes the HEAD of the checkout it runs from, so it runs from the lane's own worktree, and the run's `headSha` reads before its colour. The lane's verdict reads off its branch's CI and held-out.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-launch`>>
!! The launch ~ one brief file, any lineage

* ''A brief stands as a file'' that names the mask, the ground, the ruling it enacts, the house style and the handback shape.
* ''One launcher per Codex seat'' (`tools/launch-codex.mjs`; `--dry-run` prints the argument array and spawns nothing) takes the seat directory, the model, the effort and a mode: `build` (workspace-write, network off, both tmp excludes, `-C <worktree> --add-dir <seat>`), `read` (`-s read-only -C <seat>` over an empty directory, reading the repo from there) or `research` (`codex --search exec …` ahead of `exec`, networked, only where the brief asks for the field). Every mode carries `-c approval_policy="never"` and the seat law (#/the-seat-law). Each seat's directory sits under the lane's scratch directory, since the spend script finds the lane from the rollout's cwd.
* ''A seat that needs a CI log'' receives the excerpt as a file.
* ''A cut-short seat continues by its session id'' through the launcher's resume mode, which carries the same preflight and walls and names the model again; the lead's resume sheet records that id beside the seat's directory, model, effort and head.
* ''`codex exec` stands as the shell seat.'' `codex mcp-server` left the CLI upstream; `codex mcp` connects tools to Codex and never serves Codex as a tool.
* ''A tw5 edit leaves its dist stale'': the lead builds tw5 before keyhive and stamps it before any run, or the dist guard refuses.
* ''A moved plugin source runs `witness-all`'', since genesis-parity reads DRIFT until `genesis/unbaked.json` declares the new sha or a labelled re-bake lands.
* ''A Copilot read names its target'': the branch, its head sha and the pull request, read through `git show <branch>:<path>` or the lane's own worktree, never the main tree. A paired read runs each face alone, then hands each its twin's handback.
* ''A seat that cannot write its out file'' hands back on stdout; the launcher's log keeps it.
* ''Owed probes'' (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/codex-controller-2026`>>): a `--json` event stream ending on `turn.completed` as the watch's signal, `--output-schema` for a mechanical status beside `out.md`, permission profiles in place of the flag list, and a matched A/B across Sol, a newer Sol and Luna; each lands here once measured on this host.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/provenance`>>

!! Provenance ~ each landed line names its hands

* A commit carries a `Co-Authored-By` trailer for each lineage whose work it lands: the lead's own, `Codex <model>` for a Codex seat, `GitHub Copilot <model>` for a Copilot seat's work, and the session line.
* A handback the house keeps lands verbatim as a testimony carrier, so no relaying hand rewrites it.
* Each lineage's own transcript rests where its harness keeps it; the capture plane reads them read-only (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/capture-annotation-model`>>, <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/agent-worldline`>>).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/rulings`>>
!! Rulings ~ the operator's words the body rests on

# 2026-10-16, 2026-10-19: Terra, Luna, Sol and `gpt-5.3-codex` share one lineage and count once; the lead reads every build as the second lineage.
# 2026-10-20: the battery runs once at the wave's end and CI carries the rest; 2026-10-24: the whole e2e sets run on cloud shards only.
# 2026-10-28: seven refinements bind every lane (measure or LEAN, red before fix, one e2e file locally, findings straight to the builder's lead, one writer per worktree, deterministic spend); `gpt-6-sol` never launches; Luna reads at high; no Opus builder by default; the Codex pool keeps a 20% weekly floor; "Shift the default Codex crew to Episkopos + Terra + Luna."
# 2026-10-28, Fusion: "defer integrating Fusion until after founding, I think we can sit 'inspired' by it, but we remain in control of our worn workflows per the causal-island law (inversion-of-control)."
# 2026-10-29: Fable overwatch and batch lands; the test-architecture YIN narrows the local battery and groups compatible slices into one batch.
# 2026-10-30, working contracts: the launcher carries the seat law; the lead authors judgment work; the controller owns the one watcher per seat; the hand-back runs short; lands batch with road commits behind; job-minutes make the CI currency.
# 2026-10-30, cleanup (operator's words, recorded in the controller's memory `project_wsl_vhd_disk_law.md`): "The script based cleanup reads pono, that's enforced by the script."
# 2026-10-30, Sol (operator's words, recorded in the controller's memory `feedback_mixed_model_swarm.md`): "Approve Sol on every batch head".
# 2026-10-30, feature unit: a PR lands one whole capability a person uses end to end.
# 2026-10-30, battery: the local battery always carries `node tools/evergreen-boundary.mjs` and `bash tools/witness-all.sh`; only the heavy package suites narrow.
# 2026-10-30, tiers: Fable serves as a burst, Sonnet leads by default, general-purpose subagents run Sonnet unless the roster names otherwise.
# 2026-10-31, Codex budget (operator's words): "Reduce the use of Codex slightly, budget has burned to 26% (Sol still reads critical landings)."
# 2026-10-31, readers (operator's words): "Approve Sol readers past the 20% mark. We also have a few Copilot calls left."
# 2026-10-31, Sol per train (operator's words): "We have 10% Codex left, so I approve Sol reads on whole trains landing ... not individual lanes (approve burning lower than 5% if needed, temp bump that limit to 0%)."
# 2026-10-31, test levers (operator's word): "Approved!" for provision once, held-out per train and the static witnesses.
# 2026-10-31, cloud contract (operator's words): "Approve with a Sonnet scout to cure the gh cloud uncertainties."
